WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:02.385
You can never

00:00:02.385 --> 00:00:05.955
really put your finger
on exactly what caribou are going to do.

00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:08.624
They move from one place to another

00:00:08.624 --> 00:00:11.561
and just
when I think I start to understand them,

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:15.164
they do something completely different.

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:19.368
So I just woke up and came outside

00:00:19.368 --> 00:00:21.704
to go to the bathroom with Java,
and there's like

00:00:21.704 --> 00:00:22.405
to go to the bathroom with Java,
and there's like

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.675
a couple of dozen caribou,
like sleeping on the lake right now.

00:00:32.181 --> 00:00:35.418
Gotta be quiet.

00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:40.790
Gotta be super quiet.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.192
The snow is really crunchy right now.

00:00:45.528 --> 00:00:47.764
Generally, this time of year,

00:00:47.764 --> 00:00:50.466
the caribou are all far gone.

00:00:50.466 --> 00:00:54.704
So I've been like subsisting off
of small game, squirrels, rabbits.

00:00:57.006 --> 00:00:59.242
There they are.

00:01:00.843 --> 00:01:03.346
It's like a fire [inaudible]

00:01:03.346 --> 00:01:04.113
it's like

00:01:04.914 --> 00:01:07.583
can’t really prepare for this kind of
thing they just go down so fast.

00:01:08.584 --> 00:01:09.385
Like I haven't had breakfast.

00:01:09.385 --> 00:01:11.254
I haven't had coffee.
I haven't got anything

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:15.458
And they're not losing their sleep.

00:01:15.458 --> 00:01:18.728
It's like the most incredible thing
I've seen.

00:01:22.265 --> 00:01:22.899
It's gotta be one of

00:01:22.932 --> 00:01:25.134
the hardest stalks
you can ever put on a caribou.

00:01:26.536 --> 00:01:29.705
I hear about six, 700 yards away from them

00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:39.081
Basically what happened was

00:01:39.382 --> 00:01:42.351
one of them felt uneasy and it jumped up.

00:01:42.351 --> 00:01:45.688
Herd instinct, the rest of them jump up.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.224
Some of them are still laying down.

00:01:54.096 --> 00:01:56.165
They're starting to kind of go
back down a little bit.

00:01:56.165 --> 00:01:56.966
So I'm just going to let them be at ease.

00:01:56.966 --> 00:02:01.470
They don't have the best eyesight,
so they're going to think that

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:01.970
I'm a caribou..

00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:05.541
As long
as I'm not posing any sort of threat.

00:02:07.944 --> 00:02:09.579
So why approach this?

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:10.713
Let them see me.

00:02:11.647 --> 00:02:14.417
So none of them get rattled
and jump up like they just did.

00:02:15.985 --> 00:02:18.788
So if they're all just looking at me
and I'm kind of just walking at them

00:02:18.788 --> 00:02:22.792
slowly not posing a threat,
they're going to let me walk toward them.

00:02:25.094 --> 00:02:28.898
Let's go.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.503
Holding the gun above my head.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.739
Just kind of show it's like antlers.

00:02:37.974 --> 00:02:40.610
It's the little subtle
things like that encourage

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.979
the caribou to believe
that I'm just another caribou

00:02:49.185 --> 00:02:50.887
This is the craziest stalk man.

00:02:50.887 --> 00:02:52.455
I've gone, like, 100 yards.

00:02:52.455 --> 00:02:55.258
It's freaking nerve-wracking man.

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:56.893
Try to close that gap a little bit more.

